  "id": "copaypray",
  "neon": "#78e8d0",
  "note": "CO-PAY & PRAY — the walk-in clinic, rebuilt as the curved half of Marrow Street. Its whole\nstreet elevation is a sixteen-sided drum four storeys tall, banded twice in white porcelain, on a\nporcelain plinth, with the back-of-house behind it as a shallow centred box and the plant room set\nback on the roof.\n\n⚠ A DRUM IS THE ONLY WAY TO SAY CURVED HERE, and it is cheap rather than expensive: sixteen facets\nis sixteen quads, it is radially symmetric so it reaches the same radius at every entrance facing,\nand it carries no yaw — which is the pair of properties that make an offset curve safe where an\noffset chamfered box is not.\n\n⚠ THE DETAIL SITS ON THE PLINTH, NOT ON THE BOW. A detail part mounts on a PLANE (`cy` is the wall\nit is bolted to), so anything hung on a drum is a flat panel tangent to a curve. The entrance, the\nname board and the canopy are therefore all on the plinth face, where there is a real flat wall to\nbolt them to, and the bow above is left as glass — which is also what the building would actually\nbe.\n\n⚠ THE RED CROSS STAYS. `roofCross` is this building's sign and `KIT_DECLINE` already keeps the\nderived roof hoarding off a clinic for exactly that reason; a second board up there would be an\nadvertisement standing through the hospital sign.",
  "pal": "ty_clinic",
